[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m below: it is not a cosmetic choice.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What "input" means here
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c_src/src/main.c` declares `int main(void)` and never reads `stdin`, `argv`, the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m environment, the clock or an RNG. Grepping the C source for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf|fgets|getchar|read(|argv|argc|getenv|stdin|time(|rand|clock|srand` returns
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m nothing but three unrelated `"grandchild1"`/`"grandchild2"` string literals and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m one `#include <stdint.h>`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So this executable is a fixed test driver. All of its data-dependent branch
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m classes — empty tree, single node, sibling children, deep chain, `MAX_CHILDREN`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m saturation, duplicate id, hashmap collision plus resize, leaf removal, interior
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m subtree removal, root removal, depth/height/descendant queries, path finding —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are compiled in and exercised on *every* run. There is no stdin-driven input
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m space to enumerate.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The axes that genuinely vary between invocations are process-level:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Axis | Cases covered in `tests/differential.rs` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdin contents | empty, closed fd, single line, single item with no trailing newline, blank lines, whitespace only, embedded NULs, all 256 byte values, negative and oversized numbers, a 100 kB line, a ~1.1 MB payload, and stdin as a pipe rather than a file |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | argv | none, one empty string, `-h`, `--help`, `0`, three args, `--` plus negatives, non-ASCII args, 500 args, a 60 kB arg |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | environment | inherited, completely empty, `LC_ALL=C`, `POSIX`, `C.UTF-8`, `en_US.UTF-8`, an ISO-8859-1 `LANG`, `TERM=dumb`, colour variables |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | working directory | `/`, `/tmp`, the crate dir, `c_src` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdout/stderr wiring | separate pipes, separate regular files, both merged into one file, both merged into one pipe, `/dev/null`, `/dev/full`, append-mode file with pre-existing content, closed stdout, closed stderr, both closed, pseudo-terminal |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | pipe readers | full reader (`cat`), partial readers (`head -c 1`, `head -c 100`, `head -n 1`), `wc`, and a reader that is already gone |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | repetition | 5 sequential runs, 8 concurrent runs |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | build profile | Rust `test` profile and Rust `--release` profile, both against the same C binary |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Branch coverage of the C source was measured, not guessed. A `--coverage` build
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in `/tmp` (the `c_src` tree was not touched) gives:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m main.c      lines 100.00% of 247   branches executed 100.00% of 258
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tree.c      lines  82.74% of 168   branches executed 100.00% of 100
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m hashmap.c   lines  74.36% of 117   branches executed  92.86% of 56
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every unexecuted line is listed in "Paths unreachable from this executable"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m below, together with why it cannot be reached and whether the Rust translation of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m it is faithful anyway.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `SIGPIPE` disposition — exit status differed (FIXED)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout connected to a pipe whose reader is gone, the two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m programs disagreed on exit status while producing identical (empty) output:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ c_src/build/driver   2>/dev/null | true ; echo ${PIPESTATUS[0]}
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 141
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ translation/target/release/driver 2>/dev/null | true ; echo ${PIPESTATUS[0]}
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` before
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` runs. A C program inherits the default disposition, so glibc's exit-time
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fwrite` of the buffered stdout is interrupted by `SIGPIPE` and the process dies
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from the signal (bash reports `128 + 13 = 141`). With `SIG_IGN` in force the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust write merely failed with `EPIPE`, the return value was discarded exactly as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C discards `printf`'s, and `main` went on to `exit(0)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note that stdout alone was not the whole story: because stderr is unbuffered, the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m same divergence appeared *mid-run* when stderr was the reader-less pipe, at the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first `fprintf(stderr, ...)` inside `tree_add_node`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs` now restores the default disposition as the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first thing `main` does:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fn reset_sigpipe() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     extern "C" { fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     unsafe { signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL); }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Covered by** `sigpipe_disposition_matches` and the `release/sigpipe_*` cases in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `release_profile_matches_across_the_matrix`. Both assert C and Rust agree *and*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that the status is 141, so the test cannot pass by both programs quietly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exiting 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. A test formulation that was flaky, not a program mismatch (test-side only)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The first version of the `SIGPIPE` test used bash process substitution,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"$1" > >(exec true)`. That is a race: the reader's exit competes with the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program's single exit-time flush of 1499 bytes, and the C program wins often
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m enough to be unusable — 8 consecutive runs of the same command gave
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `141 0 0 0 0 0 0 0`. The Rust program, being slightly slower to reach its flush,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m lost the race every time and reported 141 every time.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This was a measurement artefact, not a behavioural difference: both programs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behave the same for a given fd state. Replacing the construct with a pipeline
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`| true`), where the read end is reliably closed before either program flushes,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m makes the outcome deterministic — verified 20/20 runs at 141 for the C build and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m for both Rust profiles. The test now asserts the exact status rather than mere
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m agreement.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Build-configuration hazard: `assert()` with side effects
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main.c` wraps calls that have side effects inside `assert(...)`, e.g.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m assert(tree_add_node(tree, 1, 0, "root") == 0);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so `-DNDEBUG` does not merely stop checking, it deletes the work. Confirmed by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m building the same sources with `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 24,33c24
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m < [1] root
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m <   [2] child1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ...
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m > (empty tree)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust translation uses `assert!`, which is *not* removed by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `--release`, so the release Rust binary matches the asserts-enabled C binary.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The reference build (`cmake ..` with no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, which is what the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m task specifies) leaves `NDEBUG` unset, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `reference_c_build_has_asserts_enabled` fails loudly if the C binary under
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m comparison ever turns out to be an `NDEBUG` build.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Aside from `NDEBUG`, the C output is invariant to compiler settings: gcc at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-O0`, `-O1`, `-O2`, `-O3` and `-Os` all produce byte-identical stdout and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr, matching the Rust release binary. (clang was not installed on this
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m machine, so it was not exercised.)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours that were checked and already matched
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were all verified by diffing real runs, not by reading code.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **stdout buffering mode.** glibc chooses line buffering when stdout is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   terminal and full buffering otherwise; stderr is unbuffered either way. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   translation reproduces this in `src/cstdio.rs` (`is_terminal()` probe,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `BUFSIZ = 4096`, flush at end of `main`). It matters: with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `driver >file 2>&1`, the 1499 bytes of stdout sit in the buffer until `exit`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so **both stderr lines appear before the opening banner** even though they are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   printed in the middle of the run. Under a pty the order is the natural
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   interleaved one. `merged_into_one_file_matches_including_order` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `pseudo_terminal_matches` assert the byte order in both regimes, not just
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   equality.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`printf` formatting.** `%lu` on `tree_id_t` (`uint64_t`), the two-space
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   indent per depth level in `tree_print_helper`, `[%lu] %s\n`, and the trailing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   newlines all match byte for byte.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Non-ASCII literals.** The box-drawing banner, `✓` and `═` runs come out as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   identical bytes under every locale tried, including an ISO-8859-1 `LANG` and an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   empty environment — `printf` emits the source bytes without consulting the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   locale, and so does the Rust side.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Write errors are ignored.** With stdout on `/dev/full` every write fails with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `ENOSPC`; C ignores `printf`'s return value and still exits 0. The Rust code
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   discards the `io::Result` in `cstdio::write_through`, so it also exits 0 rather
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   than panicking. Same for a closed stdout (`>&-`, `EBADF`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **stderr content and ordering.** Exactly two error paths are reached, in this
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   order:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Error: Node with ID 2 already exists
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Error: Parent has maximum children
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   from `tree_add_node`'s duplicate-id check and its `MAX_CHILDREN` check.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **FNV-1a hash byte order.** The C code casts `&key` to `uint8_t *`, so it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   hashes the host byte order; the Rust code uses `to_le_bytes()`. Identical on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   x86-64. On a big-endian host both the C and the Rust would need revisiting; the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   probe order, and therefore nothing observable in this driver, would change.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Determinism.** 5 sequential and 8 concurrent runs of each program are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   byte-identical, so no address-, pid- or timing-dependence leaks into the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   output. The Rust arena-index-for-pointer substitution does not show up.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Debug vs release Rust.** `release_and_test_profile_behave_identically`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   confirms the two profiles agree, so the `test`-profile matrix really does speak
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    200[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   for the shipped `--release` binary. This is not free: the release profile sets
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    201[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `panic = "abort"` and drops `overflow-checks`, and `hashmap.rs` contains
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    202[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `deleted_count -= 1`, which would panic in debug and wrap in release if it ever
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    203[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   underflowed. It cannot: that branch requires an entry with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    204[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `occupied && deleted`, and `resize` only ever installs freshly zeroed entries
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    205[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   while resetting `deleted_count` to 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    206[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    207[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    208[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    209[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Paths unreachable from this executable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    210[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    211[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main.c` never passes a NULL pointer and never induces an allocation failure, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    212[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the following lines cannot execute in the compiled program. Each was still read
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    213[0m:[38;5;10m[49m against its Rust counterpart; the verdicts are from that reading, not from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    214[0m:[38;5;10m[49m execution, and are labelled as such.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    215[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    216[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C location | Why unreachable | Rust counterpart |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    217[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
