[38;5;252m[1m## What I found[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOne real mismatch: [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m disposition.[22m With stdout on a pipe whose reader is gone, the C program dies from the signal (status 141) while the Rust program exited 0. Cause: Rust's standard library installs [38;5;10mSIG_IGN[0m for [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m before [38;5;10mmain[0m runs, so the failed exit-time flush was silently discarded. The same divergence appeared mid-run when [3mstderr[23m was the dead pipe, because stderr is unbuffered and dies at the first [38;5;10mfprintf[0m in [38;5;10mtree_add_node[0m. Fixed in [38;5;10mtranslation/src/main.rs[0m by restoring [38;5;10mSIG_DFL[0m as the first statement of [38;5;10mmain[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Everything else already matched byte for byte, including the parts most likely to be wrong: glibc's full-vs-line buffering decision (with [38;5;10m>file 2>&1[0m both stderr lines land [3mbefore[23m the opening banner, and under a pty they interleave normally), [38;5;10m%lu[0m formatting, the UTF-8 banner under every locale, and silently-ignored write errors on [38;5;10m/dev/full[0m and a closed stdout.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Two things worth flagging[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mmain.c[0m wraps calls with side effects inside [38;5;10massert(...)[0m, so a [38;5;10m-DNDEBUG[0m build is a [3mdifferent program[23m — every [38;5;10mtree_add_node[0m disappears and [38;5;10mtree_print[0m reports [38;5;10m(empty tree)[0m. I built with [38;5;10mcmake ..[0m and no [38;5;10mCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE[0m, as specified, and added a test that fails loudly if the C binary under comparison ever turns out to be an NDEBUG build.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This executable reads no stdin and ignores argv ([38;5;10mint main(void)[0m; confirmed by grepping for [38;5;10mscanf|fgets|read|argv|getenv|stdin|rand|time[0m). Its data branches are compiled in and all run on every invocation, so the enumerable input space is process-level: stdin contents, argv, environment, cwd, and stream wiring. gcov on an instrumented copy in [38;5;10m/tmp[0m confirms 100% branch execution in [38;5;10mmain.c[0m and [38;5;10mtree.c[0m; every unexecuted line is a NULL guard or allocation-failure path, all catalogued in [38;5;10mERRORS.md[0m with its Rust counterpart.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
My first [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m test was itself flaky — bash process substitution races the reader's exit against the program's flush, and the C program won that race 7 times in 8. I replaced it with a [38;5;10m| true[0m pipeline, verified deterministic at 141 across 20 runs for all three binaries, and the test now asserts the exact status rather than mere agreement.[0m[0m
